ff41c72047c305bd","ff9529da08497753","ffac5d667339a78a","ffe96ffe7b9a1eef"],"groups":["50e033f537f7baa6","a2a6cd4efaebe66f","5077f54f1616dceb","1418e2ddd35257ca","67f2dc4f1403091c","97abbd6030c045ca","717220a93f2165f3","7077d8c6fe70e586","48eb8594a6ea0014","11b7f7740b3eb5b0","8477163eadb53e45","f2a0af4a75fb1bfe","5775bbd2865143b0","3c88a0a05e2c7d9f","6bc5ea92c754084e","4a1a99ec792c0ec0","2b87e878c83aba08","4128d01dfe56bca4","6cadd253e11a14f2","2732b9235183bf98","5db0cae8e6f982c1","aac503ae1407012a","fd84d10d293110fc","3de2ed03216f4c4c","77f4de08c4c71d08"],"findings":"The server, host check, build, docs and CLI-registration groups match their stated intent. The grading has problems in `git-end-of-options`, `ary-guards` and `ary-conveniences`. `npm test` passes (123 of 123, **verified**). I did not drive the shell in a browser, so UI claims are **read**.\n\n**Mismatches**\n\n1. **`ary-guards` against `git-end-of-options`** (**read**, from the author's own notes). `ary-guards` says that without it \"a revision starting with '-' could reach git as a flag on the single-commit path\". `git-end-of-options` says that path is already safe, because the appended `^{commit}` makes `rev-parse` reject a leading dash. The two groups contradict each other. The `ary-guards` \"if removed\" claim is overstated for that path. The `/view` range path is covered by `--end-of-options` on `rev-list`, which was already in the repo.\n\n2. **`ary-guards` is not purely defensive.** It also holds:\n   - the `limit`/`skip` clamps, which fix a real bug (`limit=0` gave an empty page claiming `more`);\n   - the 400 refusal for option-like revisions, which is validation rather than a second layer;\n   - the commit-count cap, which the author calls a product judgment.\n\n   These are different kinds of change under one label. The headers (CSP, nosniff, no-store, referrer-policy) and the 405 check are the genuinely defensive part.\n\n**Misgraded necessity**\n\n3. **`ary-conveniences` is graded incidental, but Load more is not.**\n   - The log API caps `limit` at 500, and the UI pages by 100. Without Load more, any branch with no base (such as `main`, which gets `base = null`) cannot be browsed past its newest 100 commits.\n   - Under the task's wording, \"browsing by branch and commit\", that is closer to supporting `ary-ui`.\n   - Load more is also untested and was not exercised by a script.\n   - Refresh and the deleted-branch fallback are fairly graded incidental. The fallback is about 25 lines of branching and is the only part verified in a browser.\n\n4. **`git-end-of-options` is graded defensive; it is a removal candidate.**\n   - By the author's own account it changes nothing today, and its test passes with or without it.\n   - It edits shared code (`resolveCommit`, used by every command).\n   - It raises the minimum git version for all of git-gloss, not just `ary`.\n   - Drop it, or defer it to a separate change. The `ary` server has the leading-dash check at the `revParam` layer.\n\n5. **Mixed attribution.** Part of the commit search is claimed by `ary-server`, graded required: the two-pass `git log` and hand paging in `listLog`. That code exists only for search, which the author says was added later at the user's request, and the `ary-search` summary omits it. The task's core (list branches and commits, render ranges) would work without it. Its risks are the ones the author lists: skewed-date ordering, two full log passes, and untested non-linear history.\n\n**Missing risks**\n\n6. **Cross-site requests with a correct Host header** (**read**). The Host check stops DNS rebinding. A foreign page can still fire simple GETs at `127.0.0.1:<guessed port>`, which are not blocked by the Host check. They cannot read the responses, but they trigger synchronous git work. `--port` with a well-known value makes this easier. Impact is low, but it is not mentioned.\n\n7. **Synchronous git with a broad search.** The author lists the stall, but not that search runs two full `git log` passes plus `--stdin` with every matching sha on a single thread. That can freeze `/view` as well on large repos.\n\n8. **Packaging.** `ary-build` is fairly graded required. The author notes that `package.json` `files` is not covered, so a published tarball that omits `dist/ary` would only be caught after release.\n\n**Fair as stated:** `ary-host-check` (required is defensible, and the test checks the 403 and the message), `ary-build`, `ary-register`, `reuse-exports`, `ary-docs`, `ary-tests`.\n\nVERDICT: ISSUES","verdict":"issues"}
