fe70e586","48eb8594a6ea0014","11b7f7740b3eb5b0","8477163eadb53e45","5775bbd2865143b0","3c88a0a05e2c7d9f","6bc5ea92c754084e","4a1a99ec792c0ec0","2b87e878c83aba08","4128d01dfe56bca4","6cadd253e11a14f2","2732b9235183bf98","5db0cae8e6f982c1","aac503ae1407012a","fd84d10d293110fc","3de2ed03216f4c4c","77f4de08c4c71d08"],"findings":"**Review of `git gloss ary`: mostly accurate, two grading problems and a few unlisted risks.** All findings below are **read** (from the code and annotations only). I ran nothing, so the author's \"ran it in Chromium\" and \"tests pass\" claims are unchecked by me.\n\n## Mismatches\n- **`ary-guards` / `git-end-of-options` (h14):** The author says the change is a no-op, because `^{commit}` already makes `rev-parse` reject dash-prefixed revisions. The test passes with or without it. The group is also listed as serving `ary-guards`, but it edits `resolveCommit` in `src/git.ts`, which every command uses, not just ary. The notes say the same thing in effect, so this is more mislabelling than a lie.\n- **`ary-ui` evidence:** The shell's behaviour has no automated test, and the only check is that the script parses. The evidence for this group is manual and self-reported, so it can't be re-checked from the diff.\n\n## Misgraded necessity\n- **`ary-ui` [required]:** This is oversized for \"required\". Roughly the following is polish, which the author admits is \"kept on purpose\":\n  - the deleted-branch fallback with its notice (about 20 lines in `load()`)\n  - Refresh and Load more\n  - the \"none\" base option\n  - the dark theme\n  - the two-line subjects and the marks layout\n\n  A minimal shell would be a branch picker, a list and a frame. I'd split the conveniences into a separate `incidental` group so they can be dropped or reviewed on their own.\n- **`ary-host-check` [required]:** The security argument holds, and a server serving source over HTTP needs this check. Strictly it is `defensive`, since the task didn't ask for it. I would still keep it. The \"required\" grade is acceptable, but the author should say it is required by a safety bar, not by the task.\n- **`ary-guards` [defensive]:** The grade is fair. The CSP, nosniff and 200-commit cap are defence in depth or product choices, as the author says. The leading-`-` check in `revParam` is partly redundant with `--end-of-options`.\n- **`ary-search` [supporting]:** The grade is fair. About 110 lines of combobox plus the server-side two-pass search is a lot of weight for a convenience, but the user asked for it.\n\n## Removal candidates\n- **`git-end-of-options` (h14):** Drop or defer it. It changes behaviour for no visible gain. It also raises the minimum git version for every command: older gits would fail all revision resolution, as the author's own risk list admits. The leading-`-` check in `revParam` already covers the ary path.\n- **Search double-pass (`listLog`):** A cheaper option is a single `--grep` search with `--all-match`, or just message search. The two-pass merge adds ordering quirks (date-sorted results can differ from the normal log), a second full `git log`, and hand-paging. It is documented, but it is the most complex part of the server relative to its value.\n\n## Simplifications\n- `limit=0` is accepted by `intParam`. It makes `listLog` run `-n 1` and return an empty page with `more: true`, so a client could loop on Load more. Clamp `limit` to at least 1.\n- `notedCommits` is read for both notes refs on every `/api/log` call. Caching per request, or looking up only the shas in the page, would help large repos. The author lists this risk.\n\n## Missing risks (not in the author's list)\n- **Host check is case-sensitive on `host`.** Browsers lowercase it, so this is minor, but a client sending `LOCALHOST:port` gets a 403.\n- **HEAD requests render the full page.** `/view` runs git and builds the whole page for a HEAD request, which Node then discards.\n- **The `/view` frame and `/api` share an origin and the frame is unsandboxed.** The a