53","f99b6c83f10c8300","fa9df8b3931cd06f","faea8e39906200dc","fba89590100e26a0","fc1f9c34df87358d","fd441caa8d75bfbc","fdd26d6ec947819d","fdd43c2c69479a0f","fe153da25a83aad8","feafb49d5d9438fb","feb666c10477519e","feb98991c3a270a9","ff41c72047c305bd","ff9529da08497753","ffe96ffe7b9a1eef"],"groups":["50e033f537f7baa6","9b9ea197c8d260e6","4580ed2a94e68ca8","46911087877bf111","ed0ae7b7d467be90","bb08665afe2ef87f","717220a93f2165f3","a90d57b27de347c5","10f6873eebcffb90","11b7f7740b3eb5b0","5775bbd2865143b0","3c88a0a05e2c7d9f","6bc5ea92c754084e","2732b9235183bf98","4a1a99ec792c0ec0","2b87e878c83aba08","4128d01dfe56bca4","3de2ed03216f4c4c","77f4de08c4c71d08"],"findings":"**Overall:** the annotations are mostly accurate and the change is lean. I ran `node --test test/ary.test.ts` and all 6 tests pass (**verified**). I did not drive the browser UI, so every UI claim below is **read** only.\n\n**Mismatches and misgrading**\n\n1. **`ary-guards` [defensive]: undergraded in part (read).**\n   - The group mixes real security with product tuning. The `frame-ancestors 'self'` and CSP headers, and the nosniff header, are on the same footing as `ary-host-check`. The shell and `/view` CSPs are part of what makes serving repo content safe, so they are not optional hardening.\n   - The 405 for non-GET and the `revParam` option check are also security-relevant. The notes admit the single-commit path relies on the `revParam` check.\n   - The 200-commit cap and the `limit`/`skip` clamps are the genuinely defensive or product-judgment pieces.\n   - Suggestion: split the group, or relabel the security headers as `required` under the same reasoning the author used for the host check.\n\n2. **`ary-host-check` [required]: label fine, scope overstated (read).**\n   - The check is correct and tested.\n   - The allowlist is built from `port()`. That works because `boundPort` is set before any request arrives.\n   - With `--port 0` the check uses the real bound port, which is right.\n   - A client that sends no Host header (HTTP/1.0) gets 403. That is acceptable.\n\n3. **`ary-ui` [required]: partly overgraded (read).**\n   - The notes say Refresh, Load more, the \"none\" base option and the stale-hash fallback are \"conveniences rather than necessities\". They sit inside a `required` group.\n   - The theme/dark-mode CSS and the `Whole branch` row logic are also extras.\n   - None of this is harmful, but the group is bigger than the task strictly needs.\n   - \"Required\" holds only for the minimal shell: the commit list, the revision box and the frame.\n\n4. **`ary-search` [supporting]: accurate (read).** It was added at the user's request, and the grading and explanation are honest. The picker (~110 lines) is the largest piece of non-essential code.\n\n**Missing or understated risks**\n\n- **Search is unbounded (read).** `listLog` with a query runs two full `git log` passes. It then feeds every matching sha to `--stdin` and slices in JS, so a broad term like a single letter on a big repo loads and parses the entire history on every keystroke, after a 250ms debounce. The author lists \"feel it\", but \"Load more\" repeats the full work each time, so it is O(n) per page.\n- **Ordering claim is slightly off (read).** The comment says the union is listed \"in log order\", but `--no-walk=sorted` orders by commit date. That can differ from the default `git log` order when commit dates are skewed. Search results and unfiltered results could therefore order differently. The tests only use linear histories.\n- **`/api/log` has no cap on `head`/`base` shape beyond the leading `-` check (read).** Ranges like `a...b` or `^x` pass `revParam`, which is fine. A very large `--grep` input is capped at 200 characters, which is fine.\n- **Unsandboxed same-origin frame (read).** The author already lists this. The host check and the nonce CSP mitigate it. A hostile commit that gets script execution can read the whole repo via the API, so the blast radius is the full repo, not j