`) passes `${rev}^{commit}` to `rev-parse` with no `--end-of-options`, so the check is the only protection there. The notes admit this. The group is graded defensive, but a missing check there would be a real flag-injection bug. It deserves \"required\", or the single-commit path should be hardened in `git.ts`, which this change doesn't touch.\n- **ary-server** says \"Browsing writes nothing\", which holds for this code. `buildPageData` and `resolveCommits` are existing code I didn't audit for writes, such as notes migration or caches. The test only shows that `wip` isn't snapshotted.\n- **ary-build** is described as checked by a test. That test is a regex over `build.mjs` and `server.ts`, not a build. It would miss a `cpSync` path typo. The author says this too.\n\n**Misgraded necessity**\n- **ary-ui [required]** is overstated. The task needs a way to pick a branch, a commit and a range. Refresh, Load more, the \"none\" base, the deleted-branch fallback and the notice, the two-line subjects and the dark theme are all conveniences. The author admits some of this and says they were kept on purpose. It should be split into a required core and incidental polish. I'd call the polish incidental, because the reviewer cannot tell which of the roughly 400 lines of JS and 115 lines of CSS the task needs.\n- **ary-search [supporting]** is about 110 lines of combobox, server-side commit search and a debounce. It is requested scope, so supporting is fair. It is also the main removal candidate if size matters. The search is the riskiest code here:\n  - It runs `git log` twice and then a third time with `--stdin`.\n  - Its ordering can differ from the plain log.\n  - It re-reads the notes lists on every call.\n- **ary-host-check [required]** is correctly graded. It is the only guard against DNS rebinding.\n- **ary-tests, ary-docs, ary-register and reuse-exports** are fairly graded.\n\n**Removal candidates**\n- Search and the pickers (**ary-search**) could be deferred, and native `<select>` elements used first. Cost: poor usability on repos with hundreds of branches, which the author saw.\n- The 200-commit cap in **ary-guards** could be dropped. The author says it is a product call. The API works without it, but a huge range would hang the browser.\n\n**Simplifications**\n- The two-pass `--grep` / `--author` merge, with hand-rolled paging, could be replaced by `git log --grep --author --any-match` if the supported git has it. Check the minimum git version before relying on this.\n- `notedCommits` shells out to `git notes list` once per ref on every `/api/log` call. Caching per request, or per `refs` load, would help large repos. The author lists this as a risk.\n\n**Missing risks**\n- **Port-in-Host check:** an SSH tunnel or a Docker or devcontainer port forward on a different port is refused with 403. The author mentions the tunnel case.\n- **Host header casing and form:** the check is an exact string match on the `Host` header. A `Host: localhost` without a port, or `LOCALHOST:port`, is refused. Browsers send the port when it is non-default, so this is probably fine.\n- **Shell CSP vs view CSP:** the `/view` response sets `frame-ancestors 'self'` as a header, while the page also carries its own nonce CSP in a meta tag. Both policies apply, so tightening either one later could break the other.\n- **Synchronous git:** a slow `/view` blocks every other request, including the sidebar. The author lists this.\n- **Error text:** git errors reach the client as text. They can include local paths, but the server is loopback-only.\n- **Same-origin frame:** the view page is framed unsandboxed and same-origin with the read-only API, as the author notes. It only matters if escaping fails, but then the whole repo is readable.\n\n**ary-docs, ary-register, reuse-exports:** accurate and lean.\n\nVERDICT: ISSUES","verdict":"issues"}
