b1","f493cf443ffff50c","f4a77afbf1e36fca","f513f3da802a0b71","f5a195f66a1d374a","f5f04a5afa730b61","f61b68f91e71cc2d","f65d6d8790c396c7","f6b0ec843509b5de","f70ee4d4af56f96d","f72bb24a15b1d5d4","f73f4d5a06afae18","f740fe4ee3f0b1b7","f77af29e2ae1db28","f77af29e2ae1db28","f7dee81d46663c9c","f93815912b868f9f","f945d5fded36f853","f99b6c83f10c8300","fa9df8b3931cd06f","faea8e39906200dc","fba89590100e26a0","fc1f9c34df87358d","fd441caa8d75bfbc","fdd26d6ec947819d","fdd43c2c69479a0f","fe153da25a83aad8","feafb49d5d9438fb","feb666c10477519e","feb98991c3a270a9","ff41c72047c305bd","ff9529da08497753","ffe96ffe7b9a1eef"],"groups":["50e033f537f7baa6","9b9ea197c8d260e6","28fc886b7245d26b","696c64d6126ae1c5","8edf1bda596e3543","bb08665afe2ef87f","717220a93f2165f3","2537bcbf7174feaa","4fb15b5d8b77ed73","11b7f7740b3eb5b0","5775bbd2865143b0","3c88a0a05e2c7d9f","6bc5ea92c754084e","2732b9235183bf98","4a1a99ec792c0ec0","2b87e878c83aba08","4128d01dfe56bca4","6cadd253e11a14f2","3de2ed03216f4c4c","77f4de08c4c71d08"],"findings":"**Review of `git gloss ary`** (the full test suite passes: 122/122, **verified** by running `npm test`. Everything else below is **read** unless noted.)\n\n**Mismatches**\n\n1. **`ary-ui`: the notes overstate what it covers.**\n   - The notes say a \"stale-hash fallback\" exists. In `load()` (h6), a hash `head` is trusted as-is: `state.head = h.head || ...`. A hash naming a deleted branch gives a 422 from `/api/log` and an error in the list. Nothing falls back to the current branch. **read**\n   - The notes also say \"Refresh, Load more, the 'none' base option\" are conveniences. They are inside a group graded `required`.\n   - The author admits the group is bigger than a minimal shell, so the grade is generous. About half of it is polish: Refresh, Load more, the two-line subject clamp, the marks, the dark theme, and the `Whole branch` row logic. A minimal shell would be a few hundred fewer lines.\n2. **`ary-search`: it is not self-contained.** The picker lives in the `ary-search` hunks, but `ary-ui` calls `picker()`, `branchGroups()` and `parts.search` unconditionally. \"Supporting\" is right for the feature, but the shell can't run without these hunks. The split is a presentation boundary, not a removal boundary, so the \"if removed\" claim (\"browse without it\") is false as the code stands. **read**\n3. **`ary-host-check`: the evidence is thinner than the grade.**\n   - The `[::1]:port` entry is dead code, because the server listens only on 127.0.0.1.\n   - The `Host` check only stops DNS rebinding. It does nothing about a cross-origin `GET` that doesn't read the response, but the server is read-only, so that is fine.\n   - Calling it `required` is defensible, but it is a guard like the others. The group's own notes argue it was split out for visibility, not because the task needs it.\n\n**Misgraded necessity**\n\n- `ary-register` and `reuse-exports` are labelled `supporting` and `mechanical`. That fits. `ary-register` is `mechanical` in practice: two lines with no design content.\n- `ary-guards` is labelled `defensive`, and that is fair. `revParam`'s leading-`-` check is the only defence on the single-commit path in `viewPage`, and the notes say so. If the single-commit path doesn't use `--end-of-options`, it is effectively required. Check `resolveCommits` in `git.ts`, which this diff doesn't show.\n- `ary-build` is labelled `supporting` but is `required` for the published package. The author concedes nothing runs the build or loads from `dist/`.\n\n**Removal candidates**\n\n- Cheap to defer: Refresh, Load more, the `none` base option, the `[::1]` Host entry, `HEAD` method support, and the `referrer-policy` header. None of these changes the task.\n- The 200-commit cap is a product choice that the notes also admit is not strictly needed.\n\n**Simplifications**\n\n- The search in `listLog` runs git twice and merges the results by hand. It then pages the merged list by hand because `--no-walk` ignores `-n`. That is a lot of machinery for a search box.\n  - A single `git log --all-match --grep=Q` can't express OR between message and author.\n  - A simpler option is search by message only, with `-n`/`--skip`. That would drop the `--no-walk=sorted` ordering quirk and the second git pass.\n- `notedCommits` runs `git notes list` twice on every `/api/log` call, even when paging. Caching per request, or only listing the shas in the returned page, would cut the cost on large repos.\n\n**Missing risks the author didn't list**\n\n1. **The search covers one branch only.** `listLog`'s search takes `shas` from `range`, but the `skip`/`limit` pagination happens after a full scan. A broad term on a big repo reads the whole history on every \"Load more\", and the paging cost grows with `skip`. The author lists this risk only loosely (\"large repos will feel it\").\n2. **No request concurrency or timeout.** Git runs synchronously, so one slow `/view` blocks the whole UI. The author lists this one.\n3. **The `head` and `base` params can be ranges.** `revParam` accepts any non-dash string. A `base` such as `a..b` gives `a..b..head`, which git rejects, so it ends as a 422 with git's text in the JSON error. That is harmless, but the error text can leak local paths. The UI shows `err.message` through `textContent`, so it is safe from XSS.\n4. **The frame is same-origin and unsandboxed.** The author lists this. The `/view` page carries its own nonce CSP, so the risk depends on that CSP holding.\n5. **Port in the Host check.** If a user runs behind an SSH port-forward or a different `localhost` alias, the Host check returns a plain 403 with no hint. That is a usability gap for the main defence.\n\n**Overall:** the code does what the intent says, and the tests pass. The grading is generous on `ary-ui`, and the split between `ary-ui` and `ary-search` hides a real dependency. The author's own risk list is mostly honest.\n\nVERDICT: ISSUES","verdict":"issues"}
